From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 02:13:19 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/43] =?UTF-8?q?test(chexc):=20inference=20lambda-boundar?=
 =?UTF-8?q?y=20case=20(=C2=A76.3.2=20discharge=20by=20literal)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Pins both directions: a literal whose expected type carries the capability
discharges the requirement (no propagation to the enclosing declaration), while
a literal without one propagates it by capture. The helper takes a fallback
value rather than calling f() from its catch block, which would be outside the
try's capability scope and would make the helper itself require the capability.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../inference/infer_lambda_boundary.cj        | 63 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 63 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 testsuites/LLT/compiler/CheckedExceptions/inference/infer_lambda_boundary.cj

diff --git a/testsuites/LLT/compiler/CheckedExceptions/inference/infer_lambda_boundary.cj b/testsuites/LLT/compiler/CheckedExceptions/inference/infer_lambda_boundary.cj
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..4663391877
--- /dev/null
+++ b/testsuites/LLT/compiler/CheckedExceptions/inference/infer_lambda_boundary.cj
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+// Checked exceptions (§6.3.2, discharge by a function literal): a requirement raised
+// inside a literal whose EXPECTED TYPE carries the corresponding capability parameter
+// is discharged there and must NOT be inferred for the enclosing declaration. A literal
+// whose expected type carries no capability list discharges nothing, so the requirement
+// propagates out by capture (§2.1.2) and IS inferred.
+
+// EXEC: %compiler %cmp_opt --experimental --enable-checked-exceptions %n.cj -o %output
+// EXEC: %run %output
+
+class IOException <: Exception {}
+class NegativeException <: Exception {}
+
+// the literal parameter DOES declare a capability list: it handles IOException itself.
+// Note the fallback: calling `f()` again from the catch block would be outside the try's
+// capability scope (§3.3) and would make this helper itself require the capability.
+func withIOException<R>(f: () throws IOException -> R, fallback: R): R {
+    try {
+        f()
+    } catch (e: IOException) {
+        fallback
+    }
+}
+
+// the literal parameter declares NO capability list (a plain higher-order function)
+func apply<T, R>(x: T, f: (T) -> R): R {
+    f(x)
+}
+
+// inferred: EMPTY — the throw is covered by the literal's own capability parameter,
+// so nothing propagates here and no clause is required of this declaration
+func dischargedByLiteral(): Int64 {
+    withIOException<Int64>({ => throw IOException() }, 7)
+}
+
+// inferred: NegativeException — the literal has no capability parameters, so the
+// requirement is captured from the enclosing scope and propagates out
+func propagatesThroughLiteral(x: Int64): Int64 {
+    apply<Int64, Int64>(x) {
+        v =>
+        if (v < 0) {
+            throw NegativeException()
+        }
+        v
+    }
+}
+
+// a caller of the first needs NO handler; a caller of the second does
+func usesDischarged(): Int64 {
+    dischargedByLiteral()
+}
+
+main() {
+    let a = usesDischarged()
+    try {
+        let b = propagatesThroughLiteral(-1)
+        if (a + b == 0) {
+            return 1
+        }
+        return 1
+    } catch (e: NegativeException) {
+    }
+    return 0
+}
